<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isar is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, nearly 300 km long, rising in the Karwendel range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and flowing north-east past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> near Deggendorf. Its lower valley formed the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isar.jpg" alt="Isar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florian Schütz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isar_River_in_the_north_of_Munich.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
